  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThis approach leverages multi-resolution optimization with basis pursuit and probabilistic restarts to find a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. Unlike the provided parent implementation that heavily relies on local search, this method systematically explores the solution space by:\n\n- Using multiple resolutions (360, 720, 1440 points)\n- Projecting solutions onto a low-dimensional Fourier basis\n- Implementing a hierarchical optimization structure\n- Using probabilistic restarts to escape local optima\n- Maintaining a knowledge base of high-quality segments across resolutions\n\nThe core innovation is the structured basis pursuit optimization that replaces random search, leveraging the periodic correlation structure of the problem while avoiding local search pitfalls. The method also includes fallback mechanisms to preserve the provided parent solution as a baseline.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nAt resolution n (initially 360, can be up to 1440):\n\n- Observe h(x) = \u03a3_{k=1}^{K} (A_k * cos(\u03c9_k*x) + B_k) where K=5 basis components\n- Optimization dimensions: (A1,\u03c91),(A2,\u03c92),(A3,\u03c93),(A4,\u03c94),(A5,\u03c95) = 10 parameters\n- Constraints handled via:\n  * Soft projection: h_clip = max(0, min(1, h_raw))\n  * Integral adjustment: scale factor s = (n/2)/sum(h_clip)\n- Objective: C5(h) = (2/n) * max_k |integral h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx|\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nInitialize:\n- Start_time = time.time()\n- Remaining_budget = 1100s\n- Phase duration: 30% low-resoptim (360), 40% mid-resopt (720), 30% high-resfinetune (1440)\n- rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n- Best_c5 = float('inf')\n- Knowledge_base = []  # Stores tuples (h,params) for top candidates\n\nMain phases:\n\nPhase 1: Low resolution optimization (n=360)\n-Generate 5 candidates via Fourier basis with random parameters\n-Optimize each with:\n  * Local search: L-BFGS-B on clipped and scaled function\n  * Check constraints and update best_candidate\n-Terminate this phase when either time exhausted or minimal improvement\n\nPhase 2: Medium resolution\n-Transfer best solution to n=720 via interpolation\n-Iterate: 3x optim exp (300s total)\n-Each iteration:\n  + 5-minute check for overall time\n  + 90s to train/validate model\n  + 270s local optimization\n  + Update knowledge base if significant improvement\n-Include 10% probabilistic restarts (triggered by degradation)\n-Adjust temperature schedule: T(t) = T0 * (Tf)^{t/max_steps}\n\nPhase 3: High resolution\n-Interpolate best solution to n=1440\n-Run 1-density contrast optimization (30 minutes budget)\n-High learning rate phase (0-20% of budget)\n-Moderate phase (20-80%)\n-Gradient boost (80-100%)\n-Implement multi-candidate pool\n\nParameters:\n\u03c9: center frequencies uniformly sampled from [0.1, 3.0]\nA: amplitudes clipped to [0.1, 0.9]\nPeriodicity parameter r: expires after 75% grid size\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n- Primary basis: Fourier components (K=5)\n  * Alternative: Chebyshev basis if budget allows sweep testing\n  * Hyperparameter sweep: 3 simultaneous temperature schedules\n- Initial generation:\n  * Balanced between random and traced similarity to parent\n  * Directly shaped from provided initial_h_values for warm start\n- Optimization parameters:\n  * Initial restart span: 90% grid values\n  * Temperature decay rate: min scaling by 0.92\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n- Allocation: 300s learning, 500s optimization, 300s convergence\n- Knowledge base: verify constraints twice per candidate\n- Fallback: Store initial best candidate\n- Detection: Monitor mean improvement rate (MIR) vs phase\n- Memory: Track last 3 valid top-10 candidates per resolution\n- Validation: Re-evaluate top candidate before return\n- Projection: Sequence of clipping then gradient projection then scale\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n1. Before optimization:\n   - np.all(0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i)\n   - np.isclose(np.sum(h)*dx, 1, rtol=1e-4), where dx=2/n\n2. For final candidate:\n   - Return (h.tolist(), c5_value, n)\n   - Verify objective function result is finite\n   - Final cleaning: clip and enforce sum\n\nImplementation include progressive dimension reduction, basis projection, gradient descent with line search, and adaptive learning rate.",
